--- machine\system\services
canonical: Machine\System\Services

Every service this machine knows about. One subkey per service, named by
the service; the values inside it are the definition. peinit reads the
whole subtree once at boot to build the dependency graph, and again on
`reload-config`; between those it works from an in-memory copy and learns
about edits through a registry watch on this key.

A change is therefore **picked up, not pushed** — and when it takes
effect depends on the field, which is what each value's `applies` says.
`reload-config` is the explicit, atomic way to make peinit re-read
everything: it builds and validates a whole new graph and swaps it in only
if validation passes, without disturbing running services.

peinit creates this key on first boot if it is absent, so a fresh image
has somewhere to install into.

Default security: inherits the Machine hive root (SYSTEM and
Administrators full, Authenticated Users read). Who may manage a given
service is a separate question, answered by that service's
`ServiceSecurity`.

--- machine\system\services schemaversion
canonical: Machine\System\Services SchemaVersion
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1 (stamped by peinit when absent)
valid: 1
applies: n/a

The version of the service-definition schema in this subtree.

peinit stamps `1` on first boot if the value is absent, and never
overwrites one that is present, so a future migration that bumps it is not
silently downgraded by an older peinit booting once. A version newer
than peinit supports is a warning, not a refusal: peinit logs it and
carries on decoding what it understands, because refusing to boot over a
schema bump would be a worse failure than ignoring a field.

--- machine\system\services\<name>
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name>

One service definition. The key name is the service name, and the values
in it are the whole definition — only `ImagePath` is required.

A service name is 1 to 128 bytes of ASCII letters, digits, `.`, `_` and
`-`. Nothing else: no spaces, no slashes, no colons. The same grammar
governs a `Provides` role, because virtual and real names share one
namespace.

Unknown values are ignored, so a field from a newer schema costs nothing.
A value that is recognised but malformed is an error for the whole
definition, not a field that falls back to its default — a service whose
`Identity` you typoed must not quietly start as `LocalService`.

When an edit takes effect is per field, and every value below says so in
`applies`:

- `restart` — only a fresh process picks it up. What the process **is or
  runs as**: `ImagePath`, `Type`, `Identity`, `RequiredPrivileges`,
  `ErrorControl`.
- `next start` — the graph is rebuilt, but a running service is left
  alone: the dependency fields and the start-time checks.
- `live` — peinit consults it the next time it acts. Timeouts, restart
  policy, health checks, the hooks, and everything else.

`ServiceSecurity` is the one re-read on every control request, so an
access-control change binds on the very next command.

--- machine\system\services\<name> imagepath
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> ImagePath
type: REG_SZ
default: (required)
valid: an absolute path
applies: restart

The service binary. The one required value in a definition.

Must begin with `/`. It is the path peinit executes, not a command line —
arguments belong in `Arguments`, and there is no shell, no `PATH` search
and no word splitting here.

--- machine\system\services\<name> arguments
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> Arguments
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none)
valid: any strings; each element is one argument
applies: live

Arguments passed to `ImagePath`, one list element per argument.

Because each element is one argument, an element containing spaces arrives
as a single argument rather than being split — which is what you want for
a path with a space in it, and what catches people who write the whole
command line into one element.

--- machine\system\services\<name> workingdirectory
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> WorkingDirectory
type: REG_SZ
default: /
valid: a non-empty absolute path
applies: live

The working directory the service starts in.

--- machine\system\services\<name> ttypath
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> TTYPath
type: REG_SZ
default: (none — no terminal)
valid: an absolute path, or empty for none
applies: live

A terminal to attach as the service's stdio and controlling terminal,
e.g. `/dev/console` or `/dev/tty1`.

Absent or **empty** means the daemon default: `/dev/null` on stdin, output
captured and forwarded to the log. Empty is meaningful rather than
malformed, so a seeded or inherited `TTYPath` can be switched off without
deleting the value.

Attaching a terminal suppresses log capture — the output goes to the
terminal instead, because it cannot go to both.

--- machine\system\services\<name> ttyprecedence
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> TTYPrecedence
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0
valid: any; rejected without a TTYPath
applies: live

Who wins the terminal when several services want it at the same moment.

Higher wins; ties break on service name, so the outcome is stable rather
than a race. Setting it without a `TTYPath` is refused rather than
ignored: it can only be a mistake, and a silently inert value is one
nobody finds.

--- machine\system\services\<name> environment
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> Environment
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none)
valid: KEY=VALUE per element; KEY non-empty, no NUL
applies: live

Environment variables added to the service's environment.

Each element is split at the **first** `=`, so a value may contain further
`=` signs. An element with no `=` at all is an error. These are added to
the machine-wide defaults under `Init\EnvVars`, and win where both name
the same variable.

--- machine\system\services\<name> runtimedirectories
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> RuntimeDirectories
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none)
valid: one bare name per element; no /, \, ., .. or controls
applies: live

Directories created directly under `/run` just before the main process
starts.

Each element is a plain relative name — `foo`, never `foo/bar` and never
`/run/foo`. For `foo`, peinit creates `/run/foo` and applies a descriptor
granting full access to SYSTEM, Administrators and this service's own SID.
If it cannot create or secure it, the start fails with
`ParentSetupFailure`.

peinit does **not** remove these when the service stops. `/run` is a
boot-scoped tmpfs and the next boot clears it for you.

--- machine\system\services\<name> limitnofile
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> LimitNOFILE
type: REG_DWORD
default: (inherited from peinit)
valid: any
applies: live

`RLIMIT_NOFILE` for the service: how many file descriptors it may hold.

--- machine\system\services\<name> limitcore
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> LimitCORE
type: REG_DWORD
default: (inherited from peinit)
valid: any, in bytes
applies: live

`RLIMIT_CORE` for the service, in bytes: the largest core dump it may
write. `0` disables core dumps for this service.

--- machine\system\services\<name> type
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> Type
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0 (Simple)
valid: 0 or 1
applies: restart

What shape of process this is.

- `0` — **Simple**: a long-running process. It is expected to stay up, and
  its exit is an event the restart policy answers.
- `1` — **Oneshot**: a process that does a job and exits. A successful
  exit is the normal outcome, not a failure.

Any other value refuses the definition rather than falling back to Simple.

--- machine\system\services\<name> readiness
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> Readiness
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0 (Notify)
valid: 0 or 1
applies: live

How peinit decides the service is ready, for Simple services.

- `0` — **Notify**: wait for `READY=1` over the notify socket. The service
  says when it is ready, which is the only way to be right about it.
- `1` — **Alive**: ready as soon as the process is running. For a service
  that cannot be taught to notify.

Ignored for Oneshot, where readiness is the exit.

--- machine\system\services\<name> remainafterexit
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> RemainAfterExit
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0
valid: 0 or 1
applies: live

Oneshot only: stay Completed after a successful exit rather than going
inactive.

What it is for is dependencies — a Oneshot that sets something up stays
"done" so services that `Requires` it keep their satisfaction after it has
exited.

--- machine\system\services\<name> successexitcodes
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> SuccessExitCodes
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none — only 0 is success)
valid: decimal 0-255 per element; duplicates ignored
applies: live

Non-zero exit codes to treat as success.

For the programs that report something other than failure through a
non-zero status — "nothing to do", "already applied". Without this every
non-zero exit is a failure and the restart policy acts on it.

--- machine\system\services\<name> triggers
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> Triggers
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none — demand-only)
valid: boot, boot:settled, tty:released, timer:<schedule>
applies: live

What starts this service without anybody asking.

- `boot` — start it as part of the boot set.
- `boot:settled` — start it once the boot set has stopped moving, bounded
  by `Boot\SettleTimeout`. For things that should not compete with boot.
- `tty:released` — start it when the terminal named by `TTYPath` is given
  up by whoever held it. Refused without a `TTYPath`.
- `timer:<schedule>` — start it on a schedule. See `TimerPersistent`,
  `TimerJitter` and the `TimerState` subkey.

Absent means demand-only: it starts when something starts it. Bare `tty`
and bare `timer` are errors, not shorthands, and an unrecognised
sub-trigger (`boot:setled`) is an error rather than a silently unknown
kind.

--- machine\system\services\<name> disabled
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> Disabled
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0
valid: 0 or 1
applies: live

If `1`, triggers must not activate this service.

It does not make the service unstartable — an administrator can still
start it by hand, and a dependency still pulls it in. It switches off the
automatic paths only, which is the difference between "off" and "gone".

--- machine\system\services\<name> safemode
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> SafeMode
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0
valid: 0 or 1
applies: live

If `1`, attempt to start this service in Safe mode as well as normal boot.

`ErrorControl = 1` (Critical) implies it: a service the machine cannot
boot without is by definition one Safe mode needs.

--- machine\system\services\<name> conditions
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> Conditions
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none)
valid: path:, file:, directory: or registry: with an argument
applies: next start

Start-time checks whose failure **skips** the service.

A skip is not a failure: nothing goes red, dependants are not failed, and
the next trigger tries again. Use it for "only if this applies to this
machine".

A `registry:` check may only name a key under `Machine\System\Services` or
`Machine\System\Init` — the two subtrees peinit already holds in memory.
Anything else is refused, because evaluating it would mean a synchronous
registry read in the middle of a start.

--- machine\system\services\<name> asserts
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> Asserts
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none)
valid: same grammar as Conditions
applies: next start

Start-time checks whose failure **fails** the service.

Same checks as `Conditions`, opposite verdict: this is for a precondition
whose absence means something is wrong, not something is inapplicable.

--- machine\system\services\<name> prestartchecktimeout
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> PreStartCheckTimeout
type: REG_DWORD
default: 5
valid: seconds
applies: live

Seconds allowed for the helper that evaluates filesystem `Conditions` and
`Asserts`.

If it overruns it is killed and the check counts as **not satisfied** — so
a Condition skips and an Assert fails. Erring that way is deliberate: a
check that did not answer is not a check that passed.

--- machine\system\services\<name> timerpersistent
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> TimerPersistent
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1
valid: 0 or 1
applies: live

Whether a missed timer run is caught up after a reboot.

With `1`, a machine that was off when the timer should have fired runs it
once on the next boot, using the timestamp in `LastTimerRun` or
`TimerState`. With `0`, a missed firing is simply missed.

--- machine\system\services\<name> timerjitter
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> TimerJitter
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0
valid: seconds
applies: live

Maximum random delay added to each firing, in seconds.

The delay is drawn fresh per firing in `0..=TimerJitter`. It exists to
stop a fleet configured from one image from hitting the same server in the
same second; on a single machine it has no purpose.

--- machine\system\services\<name> identity
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> Identity
type: REG_SZ
default: LocalService
valid: a principal name or SID; empty means the default
applies: restart

The principal the service token is minted for.

A well-known name (`SYSTEM`, `LocalService`, `NetworkService`) or a
literal SID. Whatever it is, the token also carries the `S-1-5-80` service
SID derived from the service name, which is how a record under
`Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy` can grant a privilege to this service alone
rather than to everything running as `LocalService`.

--- machine\system\services\<name> requiredprivileges
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> RequiredPrivileges
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none — every privilege removed)
valid: privilege names; no empty element
applies: restart

The privileges the service keeps. Everything else is removed from its
token.

This is an allow-list, not an addition: the default of absent means the
service runs with none of them. Naming too few is the common mistake and
an awkward one to diagnose — `SeChangeNotifyPrivilege` in particular is
what lets a process traverse a directory to open a file, so a daemon
granted only the privilege it obviously needs may not be able to read its
own state.

--- machine\system\services\<name> hookidentity
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> HookIdentity
type: REG_SZ
default: (the service's own Identity)
valid: a principal name or SID; empty means the default
applies: live

The identity `ExecStartPre` and `ExecStartPost` run as.

For the case where the setup step needs more authority than the daemon
should keep — the hook runs as one principal and the service as another,
instead of the service holding the union for its whole life.

--- machine\system\services\<name> requires
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> Requires
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none)
valid: service or service:level; level 1-64 graphic ASCII
applies: next start

Hard dependencies: this service does not start until each of these is
satisfied, and fails if one cannot be.

An entry may name a readiness **level** — `netd:routed`,
`timed:synchronised` — to wait for a particular state rather than merely
for the service to be up. The level vocabulary belongs to the service
publishing it.

An entry may also name a role rather than a service: anything another
service lists in `Provides` is satisfied by that service.

--- machine\system\services\<name> wants
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> Wants
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none)
valid: same grammar as Requires
applies: next start

Soft dependencies: start these first if they can start, but do not fail if
they cannot.

--- machine\system\services\<name> bindsto
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> BindsTo
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none)
valid: same grammar as Requires
applies: next start

Runtime coupling: `Requires`, plus this service stops when the named
service stops.

The difference from `Requires` is what happens after a successful start.
`Requires` is a start-ordering statement; `BindsTo` keeps holding, so a
service bound to a device or a network that goes away goes away with it.

--- machine\system\services\<name> conflicts
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> Conflicts
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none)
valid: same grammar as Requires; a level is inert here
applies: next start

Mutual exclusion: starting this service stops the named ones, and they
cannot run together.

A level on a `Conflicts` entry parses but means nothing — conflicting with
a service at one readiness level and not another is not something peinit
can act on. It is accepted rather than specially rejected because a
grammar rule that holds for three of four list fields is one people get
wrong.

--- machine\system\services\<name> provides
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> Provides
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none)
valid: bare role names, service-name grammar; no level
applies: next start

Virtual names this service fills, so other services can depend on the
**role** without naming the implementation.

A role is validated against the ordinary service-name grammar because
virtual and real names share one namespace: `Requires = ["authn"]` is
satisfied either by a service literally called `authn` or by any service
providing it, and that only works while both sides admit the same strings.

A level is refused rather than ignored. `netd:routed` names a condition on
a service, and a role is not a service, so there is nothing for the level
to qualify.

--- machine\system\services\<name> onfailure
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> OnFailure
type: REG_SZ
default: (none)
valid: one service name, no level
applies: next start

A service to start when this one enters Failed.

For the recovery action — a notifier, a fallback, a cleanup. It fires on
entry to Failed, which is after the restart policy has given up, not on
each failed attempt.

--- machine\system\services\<name> errorcontrol
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> ErrorControl
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0 (Normal)
valid: 0 or 1
applies: restart

How much the machine depends on this service.

- `0` — **Normal**: its failure is its own.
- `1` — **Critical**: the machine cannot usefully run without it. An
  irrecoverable failure syncs the disks and reboots, and boot is not
  called successful until it has held a satisfying state for
  `Boot\BootSuccessGrace`. Critical implies `SafeMode`.

Reach for Critical rarely and deliberately: it converts a service fault
into a reboot loop if the fault is persistent.

--- machine\system\services\<name> restartpolicy
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> RestartPolicy
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1 (OnFailure)
valid: 0, 1 or 2
applies: live

When peinit restarts the service after it exits.

- `0` — **Never**.
- `1` — **OnFailure**: restart on a failing exit, leave a successful one
  alone. The default, and right for almost everything.
- `2` — **Always**: restart whatever the exit status. For a process whose
  clean exit still means the machine has lost the service.

--- machine\system\services\<name> restartmaxretries
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> RestartMaxRetries
type: REG_DWORD
default: 5
valid: consecutive attempts
applies: live

Consecutive restarts before the service is declared Failed.

The counter resets after the service has been healthy for
`RestartWindow` — so a service that fails once a day forever is restarted
every time, and one that fails five times in a minute is failed.

--- machine\system\services\<name> restartwindow
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> RestartWindow
type: REG_DWORD
default: 120
valid: seconds
applies: live

Seconds of healthy running that reset the restart counter.

This is what separates a flapping service from an occasionally unlucky
one, and the value worth tuning if your service takes longer than two
minutes to prove itself.

--- machine\system\services\<name> restartdelay
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> RestartDelay
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1
valid: seconds; the backoff base
applies: live

The backoff base, in seconds: how long peinit waits before the first
restart.

It doubles per consecutive failure and is capped at 60 seconds, so the
default gives 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 60, 60 …

--- machine\system\services\<name> healthcheck
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> HealthCheck
type: REG_SZ
default: (none — no health checking)
valid: an absolute executable, with arguments
applies: live

A command run periodically to decide whether the service is healthy.

The executable must be absolute. Arguments are split on whitespace, and a
double-quoted run is kept together — there is no shell, so no globbing, no
variable expansion and no pipelines. Exit `0` is healthy.

--- machine\system\services\<name> healthcheckinterval
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> HealthCheckInterval
type: REG_DWORD
default: 30
valid: seconds
applies: live

Seconds between health checks.

--- machine\system\services\<name> healthchecktimeout
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> HealthCheckTimeout
type: REG_DWORD
default: 5
valid: seconds
applies: live

Seconds before a running health check is killed and counted as a failure.

Keep it comfortably below `HealthCheckInterval`: a check that can outlive
its own interval makes the interval meaningless.

--- machine\system\services\<name> healthcheckretries
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> HealthCheckRetries
type: REG_DWORD
default: 3
valid: consecutive failures
applies: live

Consecutive failed health checks before the service is unhealthy.

--- machine\system\services\<name> watchdogtimeout
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> WatchdogTimeout
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0 (disabled)
valid: seconds; 0 disables
applies: live

Seconds peinit will wait between `WATCHDOG=1` pings before acting.

The service must send them itself over the notify socket, so this is only
useful for a service built to. It catches the hang a health check cannot:
a process that is up, answering nothing, and would pass any check that
merely looks at it from outside.

--- machine\system\services\<name> execstartpre
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> ExecStartPre
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none)
valid: an absolute executable per element, with arguments
applies: live

Commands run before the service binary, in order.

Any failure aborts the start — these are preparation, and a start that
proceeds on half-done preparation is worse than one that does not
proceed. They run as `HookIdentity` if set, otherwise as the service's own
`Identity`. Same command grammar as `HealthCheck`: absolute executable, no
shell.

--- machine\system\services\<name> execstartpost
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> ExecStartPost
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none)
valid: an absolute executable per element, with arguments
applies: live

Commands run after the service becomes ready, or after a Oneshot exits
successfully.

A failure here is logged and **not** fatal, which is the opposite of
`ExecStartPre` and deliberate: by this point the service is up, and
tearing it down because a notification step failed would trade a working
service for a tidy status.

--- machine\system\services\<name> execreload
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> ExecReload
type: REG_SZ
default: (none — SIGHUP)
valid: an absolute executable, or signal:<NAME>
applies: live

What `reload` does to this service.

Absent, peinit sends SIGHUP. `signal:SIGUSR1` sends that signal instead —
the name must be a real signal, spelled with its `SIG` prefix. Anything
else is a command, run under the same absolute-executable rule as the
other hooks.

--- machine\system\services\<name> starttimeout
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> StartTimeout
type: REG_DWORD
default: 30
valid: seconds
applies: live

Seconds for the whole start sequence — hooks, exec, and readiness
together.

Not just the binary: a service with slow `ExecStartPre` commands is
spending this budget before its own process has begun.

--- machine\system\services\<name> stoptimeout
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> StopTimeout
type: REG_DWORD
default: 10
valid: seconds
applies: live

Seconds between SIGTERM and SIGKILL when the service is asked to stop.

A service that needs to flush state on the way down needs this long enough
to do it; one that ignores SIGTERM only makes shutdown slower.

--- machine\system\services\<name> notifyaccess
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> NotifyAccess
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0 (Main)
valid: 0
applies: live

Who may send readiness and watchdog notifications for this service.

`0` (Main — the main process only) is the sole supported value; anything
else refuses the definition. The value exists so that widening it later is
a value change rather than a schema change.

--- machine\system\services\<name> fdstoremax
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> FdStoreMax
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0 (disabled)
valid: descriptors; 0 disables
applies: live

How many file descriptors peinit will hold for this service across a
restart.

For the service that wants to keep a listening socket or an open device
over a restart instead of racing to reacquire it. `0` means no store at
all.

--- machine\system\services\<name> servicesecurity
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> ServiceSecurity
type: REG_BINARY
default: (absent — inherits the parent key)
valid: a self-relative Peios security descriptor
applies: live

Who may manage this service: start it, stop it, query it.

Re-read on **every** control request rather than cached with the
definition, so a change binds on the very next command without touching
the running process. That makes it the right tool for delegating one
service to a team without handing over service control generally.

Note what it does not govern: editing the definition. That is a registry
write, gated by this key's own descriptor.

--- machine\system\services\<name> displayname
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> DisplayName
type: REG_SZ
default: (the service name)
valid: any string; empty means absent
applies: live

A human-readable name for status output.

--- machine\system\services\<name> description
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> Description
type: REG_SZ
default: (none)
valid: any string; empty means absent
applies: live

What this service is for, in a sentence, for whoever finds it in a status
listing at three in the morning.

--- machine\system\services\<name> lasttimerrun
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name> LastTimerRun
type: REG_QWORD
default: (absent until the timer has fired)
valid: written by peinit
applies: n/a

When this service's timer last fired, for a service with exactly one timer
trigger.

Written by peinit, not configuration. It is what `TimerPersistent` reads
to decide whether a firing was missed while the machine was off. A service
with several timer triggers uses the `TimerState` subkey instead, because
one value cannot record several schedules.

Deleting it makes the next boot treat the timer as never having run.

--- machine\system\services\<name>\timerstate
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name>\TimerState

Per-trigger last-run timestamps, for a service with more than one timer
trigger.

One value per schedule, so each trigger is caught up independently. Like
`LastTimerRun` this is peinit's record rather than configuration; the key
appears once the service has fired.

--- machine\system\services\<name>\timerstate <schedule>
canonical: Machine\System\Services\<name>\TimerState <schedule>
type: REG_QWORD
default: (absent until that trigger has fired)
valid: written by peinit
applies: n/a

When one particular timer trigger last fired.

The value **name** is the schedule string from `Triggers`, percent-encoded:
everything outside ASCII letters, digits, `.`, `_` and `-` becomes `%XX`
with uppercase hex. So the trigger `timer:--* 03:00:00` records against
the value name `%2A-%2A-%2A%2003%3A00%3A00`.

The encoding exists because a schedule contains characters a value name
cannot carry, and it is reversible so the two can always be matched up.

--- machine\system\boot
canonical: Machine\System\Boot

How boot and shutdown are paced. Five values, all optional, all read by
peinit at the point it needs them.

Nothing here describes what boots — that is the `Triggers` on each
service. This key is about the bounds: how much may happen at once, how
long a thing is given before peinit stops waiting for it.

Unlike `Services` and `Init`, **this key is not watched**. peinit does not
notice an edit here the way it notices one to a service definition, and
does not create the key if it is absent. Each value below says what it
actually takes: most of them the next boot, `ShutdownTimeout` an explicit
`reload-config`.

Default security: inherits the Machine hive root (SYSTEM and
Administrators full, Authenticated Users read).

--- machine\system\boot maxparallelstarts
canonical: Machine\System\Boot MaxParallelStarts
type: REG_DWORD
default: 10
valid: greater than 0; 0 sends boot to Recovery
applies: next boot

The most services peinit will have starting concurrently.

Absent means the default. **Zero is not** — a plan that may start nothing
can never finish, so rather than silently substituting the default peinit
treats it as an unbootable configuration and goes to Recovery, where you
can fix it.

Lower it on a machine whose boot is I/O-bound and thrashing; raising it
past what the storage can feed rarely helps.

--- machine\system\boot bootsuccessgrace
canonical: Machine\System\Boot BootSuccessGrace
type: REG_DWORD
default: 30
valid: seconds
applies: next boot

Seconds a Critical service must hold a satisfying state before boot counts
as successful.

The point is to catch the service that starts, reports ready, and dies
eight seconds later. Without a grace period that boot would be recorded as
good and the machine would keep booting into it.

--- machine\system\boot shutdowntimeout
canonical: Machine\System\Boot ShutdownTimeout
type: REG_DWORD
default: 90
valid: seconds
applies: reload-config

Maximum seconds for the whole graceful shutdown.

It bounds the shutdown as a whole; each service's own `StopTimeout` bounds
its part of it, and `PostKillTimeout` bounds the last stage. When this
expires peinit stops waiting and finishes the shutdown regardless.

The one value on this key that a running peinit will pick up — but only
when something makes it re-read its configuration. `Boot` is not watched,
so an edit sits there until an explicit `reload-config` or the next boot.

--- machine\system\boot settletimeout
canonical: Machine\System\Boot SettleTimeout
type: REG_DWORD
default: 5
valid: seconds
applies: next boot

Seconds to wait for the boot set to stop moving before starting
`boot:settled` services anyway.

It bounds the wait so that one service stuck in Starting delays a console
prompt rather than denying it.

--- machine\system\boot postkilltimeout
canonical: Machine\System\Boot PostKillTimeout
type: REG_DWORD
default: 5
valid: seconds
applies: next boot

Seconds peinit waits for a service's cgroup to drain after SIGKILL before
treating it as stuck.

`ShutdownTimeout` bounds the shutdown as a whole; this bounds one
service's last stage of it. A cgroup that will not empty usually means a
process in uninterruptible sleep, which no further signal will move.

--- machine\system\init
canonical: Machine\System\Init

peinit's own operational parameters: its two sockets, its logging, its
control descriptor, and the two subkeys that hold machine-wide environment
defaults and boot-time path provisioning.

Everything here is optional and has a compiled-in default, so an empty key
is a working machine. peinit watches this key along with `Services`, and
creates it on first boot if it is absent.

Note the division against `Machine\System\Boot`: this key is about how
peinit runs, that one about how boot and shutdown are paced. The practical
difference is the watch — an edit here is noticed and applied, an edit
there generally waits for the next boot.

Default security: inherits the Machine hive root (SYSTEM and
Administrators full, Authenticated Users read). The `EnvVars` subkey is
security-critical and should be tighter — see its page.

--- machine\system\init controlsecurity
canonical: Machine\System\Init ControlSecurity
type: REG_BINARY
default: SYSTEM full; Administrators shutdown and reload-config
valid: a self-relative Peios security descriptor
applies: live

Who may perform system-level control operations — shutdown, reboot,
`reload-config`.

This is the machine-wide door. Who may manage one particular service is
that service's `ServiceSecurity`, a separate descriptor with a separate
answer. Absent or malformed, the compiled default above applies.

--- machine\system\init maxcontrolconnections
canonical: Machine\System\Init MaxControlConnections
type: REG_DWORD
default: 32
valid: concurrent connections
applies: live

Maximum concurrent connections to the control socket.

--- machine\system\init maxrequestsize
canonical: Machine\System\Init MaxRequestSize
type: REG_DWORD
default: 65536
valid: bytes
applies: live

Maximum size of a single control-socket request, in bytes.

--- machine\system\init connectiontimeout
canonical: Machine\System\Init ConnectionTimeout
type: REG_DWORD
default: 30
valid: seconds
applies: live

Seconds an idle control connection is held before it is closed.

--- machine\system\init maxjobsconnections
canonical: Machine\System\Init MaxJobsConnections
type: REG_DWORD
default: 64
valid: concurrent connections
applies: live

Maximum concurrent connections to the jobs socket.

The jobs socket is the other door — where submitted work arrives — and it
is bounded separately from control because the two have different callers
and different appetites.

--- machine\system\init maxjobmessagesize
canonical: Machine\System\Init MaxJobMessageSize
type: REG_DWORD
default: 32768
valid: bytes
applies: live

Maximum size of one jobs-socket message, in bytes.

This bounds a submission's whole definition, so a submitter with a very
large `Environment` or argument list meets it first.

--- machine\system\init jobsconnectiontimeout
canonical: Machine\System\Init JobsConnectionTimeout
type: REG_DWORD
default: 30
valid: seconds
applies: live

Seconds an idle jobs connection is held before it is closed.

--- machine\system\init maxjobspersubmitter
canonical: Machine\System\Init MaxJobsPerSubmitter
type: REG_DWORD
default: 64
valid: live jobs per SID
applies: live

How many live submitted jobs one submitting SID may hold at once.

A fairness bound, so one caller cannot fill the job table and deny
everybody else. SYSTEM is exempt — the exemption exists so that the
machine's own recovery work is not blocked by a quota meant for its
callers.

--- machine\system\init maxloglinelength
canonical: Machine\System\Init MaxLogLineLength
type: REG_DWORD
default: 8192
valid: at least 256 bytes
applies: live

Maximum bytes of one service output line before it is truncated.

Below the minimum the value is ignored, the default is used, and peinit
warns. A logging knob must not be able to decide how the machine boots, so
it is kept rather than fatal; warned rather than silent, because an
operator who sets a value and sees no change has no way to tell the
setting from their diagnosis. The same rule governs the three values
below.

--- machine\system\init maxlogbufferperservice
canonical: Machine\System\Init MaxLogBufferPerService
type: REG_DWORD
default: 65536
valid: at least 4096 bytes (one page)
applies: live

Maximum bytes buffered per service output pipe before back-pressure.

The floor is real rather than judgement: this is applied with
`F_SETPIPE_SZ`, whose kernel minimum is one page and which rounds up
regardless. Asking for less is not a smaller pipe, it is the same pipe and
a misleading number in the registry.

--- machine\system\init logreadbytesperevent
canonical: Machine\System\Init LogReadBytesPerEvent
type: REG_DWORD
default: 16384
valid: at least 512 bytes
applies: live

Bytes drained from a service's output pipe per readable event.

Larger favours throughput on a chatty service; smaller favours fairness
between services, because peinit moves on to the next pipe sooner.

--- machine\system\init preeventdbuffer
canonical: Machine\System\Init PreEventdBuffer
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1048576
valid: at least 4096 bytes
applies: live

Total bytes of service output held in memory before eventd is up to
receive it.

Early boot produces log lines before the thing that stores them exists.
Overflow drops the **oldest** output and never blocks a service — a
service must not be slowed by the machine's inability to record what it
said. Raise it where early services are noisy, lower it where boot-time
memory is tight.

--- machine\system\init\envvars
canonical: Machine\System\Init\EnvVars

Machine-wide default environment variables, injected into every Phase 2
service.

The value **name** is the variable and its `REG_SZ` data is the value — no
`KEY=VALUE` packing, unlike a service's own `Environment`, which is a list
and has to. Where both name the same variable the service's own wins.

This key is security-critical and deserves a tighter descriptor than it
inherits. A variable here is read by every service on the machine,
including privileged ones, so write access to it is close to write access
to what those services do.

--- machine\system\init\envvars <variable>
canonical: Machine\System\Init\EnvVars <variable>
type: REG_SZ
default: (none)
valid: name non-empty and free of =; data any string
applies: next start

One machine-wide environment variable: the value name is the variable
name, the data is its value.

A name that is empty or contains `=` is refused — the `=` rule because a
name carrying one could not round-trip into an environment block
unambiguously.

Services already running keep the environment they were started with;
there is no way to change a live process's environment, so this applies at
the next start.

--- machine\system\init\provisionedpaths
canonical: Machine\System\Init\ProvisionedPaths

Boot-time path provisioning: directories and files peinit creates and
secures after registryd starts and before Phase 2.

One subkey per entry. This is the registry-backed equivalent of
`tmpfiles.d` — the place to declare the directory your service needs to
exist, owned correctly, before it runs.

A malformed entry is logged and skipped rather than blocking boot. Only an
entry marked `Required = 1` can send boot to Recovery, and only when it
genuinely cannot be created or secured.

--- machine\system\init\provisionedpaths\<name>
canonical: Machine\System\Init\ProvisionedPaths\<name>

One path to provision. The subkey name is a label of your choosing,
validated against the service-name grammar; the object itself is named by
`Path`.

`Kind` and `Path` are both required and an entry missing either is
skipped. Two things worth knowing before relying on this: `Kind = file`
never truncates an existing file — it only makes sure the file is
present — and peinit does **not** create missing parent directories.
Declare each directory as its own entry, or depend on the package that
owns the parent.

--- machine\system\init\provisionedpaths\<name> kind
canonical: Machine\System\Init\ProvisionedPaths\<name> Kind
type: REG_SZ
default: (required)
valid: directory or file, lowercase
applies: next boot

What to create at `Path`.

Matched exactly and in lowercase; `Directory` is not accepted. An
unrecognised kind skips the entry with a warning.

--- machine\system\init\provisionedpaths\<name> path
canonical: Machine\System\Init\ProvisionedPaths\<name> Path
type: REG_SZ
default: (required)
valid: an absolute path
applies: next boot

The path to create or verify.

--- machine\system\init\provisionedpaths\<name> security
canonical: Machine\System\Init\ProvisionedPaths\<name> Security
type: REG_BINARY
default: SYSTEM and Administrators full, users read and traverse
valid: a self-relative Peios file security descriptor
applies: next boot

The descriptor applied to the object once it exists.

It is applied whether or not peinit created the object this boot, so an
entry is also a way to keep a path's security correct rather than merely
to create it.

--- machine\system\init\provisionedpaths\<name> required
canonical: Machine\System\Init\ProvisionedPaths\<name> Required
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0
valid: 0 or 1
applies: next boot

If `1`, failing to provision this entry sends boot to Recovery before
Phase 2 starts.

Reserve it for a path without which the machine is not usable. Everything
else should be allowed to fail loudly in the log and let the rest of boot
proceed.
